WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.141 --> 00:00:03.200
- In the first volume of his 2002 memoir,

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:06.400
"Living to Tell the Tale"
or "Vivir para Contarla",

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:08.320
Colombian author and Nobel Prize

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:11.120
in Literature winner
Gabriel García Márquez

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:13.440
wrote: "Life is not what one lived,

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:15.600
but rather what one remembers,

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:18.000
and how it is remembered
to tell the tale."

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.160
And while Marquez was
specifically referring

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:21.920
to the nature of autobiography,

00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:23.680
it is hard not to hear his words

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:26.320
and specifically relate to
them the sub-genre of fiction

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:29.840
that he helped spearhead, magical realism.

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:32.800
(upbeat bright music)

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:34.800
Broadly speaking, magical realism

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.680
is a literary fiction style
that paints a realistic view

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:39.440
of the contemporary world,

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:41.520
while also adding magical elements,

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:44.400
blurring the lines between
fantasy and reality.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.800
While the label itself
is kind of nebulous,

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:47.920
professor of English,

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:50.160
Wendy Faris, mapped out several tenets

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:53.120
that she believes are the
building blocks to the genre.

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:54.560
And as luck would have it,

00:00:54.560 --> 00:00:56.320
everybody's favorite fictional author,

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:59.520
Jimmy Squibbles, is working
on his magical realist sequel

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:02.240
to his first novel,
"Hype House Bloodbath".

00:01:02.240 --> 00:01:03.360
TENET NUMBER ONE:

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:06.160
An "irreducible" magic
which cannot be explained

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:08.560
by typical notions of natural law.

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:11.600
In this case, Jimmy
Squibbles wakes up one day

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:14.640
to realize he has been
turned into a horse.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:16.000
TENET NUMBER TWO:

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.560
A realist description
that stresses normal,

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:20.720
common, every-day phenomena,

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:24.240
which is then revised or
"refelt" by the marvelous.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:27.280
Extreme or amplified
states of mind or setting

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:29.360
are often used to accomplish this.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:32.160
This distinguishes the
genre from myth or fantasy.

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:36.560
In this case, Jimmy's Hype
House roommates, Chad and Kyle,

00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:39.600
do not seem to mind that
Jimmy is now a horse,

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:42.800
continuing to film their
15-million follower vlogs

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:44.240
without his human presence,

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:45.600
and no one in the comments

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:47.360
seems to notice his absence either.

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:50.640
Jimmy lives his life as best
as he can in horse form,

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.840
but finds challenges in trying
to go lifting at the gym

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:55.600
and meeting girls at the club.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:56.880
TENET NUMBER THREE:

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:58.960
The novel causes the reader to be drawn

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:01.120
between the two views of reality.

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:02.800
The reader is meant to question

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:04.960
if Jimmy is better off as a horse,

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:07.040
freed from the constraints of celebrity,

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:10.160
or if he should find the
solution to turn himself back

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:12.080
into a YouTube superstar.

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:13.600
TENET NUMBER FOUR:

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:17.280
These two visions or realms
nearly merge or intersect.

00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:20.640
Jimmy the horse begins to see
the value in equine freedom

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:23.760
after romping the fields
with his fellow horses.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:26.640
His YouTube audience finally
discovers his secret.

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:28.160
TENET NUMBER FIVE:

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:30.720
Time is both history and the timeless;

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:32.080
space is often challenged;

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:33.760
identity is broken down at times.

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:36.880
Jimmy decides he IS the horse.

00:02:36.880 --> 00:02:40.560
He opens a Hype House stable
for his new equine friends.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:42.720
Historically, the term
was coined as a comment

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:45.040
upon the emergence of a
certain kind of realism

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:46.400
in the visual arts.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:49.200
It was first used in 1925 by art critic

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:52.800
Franz Roh to define art that
contrasted with realism,

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:55.760
that included elements
identifiable as "real",

00:02:55.760 --> 00:02:59.360
but were mixed with aspects
of the uncanny or fantastic

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:01.920
to the point where one
questions the "real".

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:04.320
From Gustave Courbet's
realist masterpiece,

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:08.640
A Burial At Ornans, wherein
everything from subject matter,

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:10.960
form and presentation
are near photographic

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:14.080
in how close to reality
they are, to something like

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:15.840
Rene Magritte's The Son of Man,

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:19.040
which juxtaposes objects
pulled from real life

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:21.920
in a manner that heightens
them to fantasy and mystery.

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:25.440
And, once again, we are
working in loose terms here.

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:28.480
As a literary movement,
however, magical realism's roots

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:29.920
are fairly global.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:31.360
Loosely, it began in Europe,

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:34.240
in the 1910s-1920s, with works like

00:03:34.240 --> 00:03:35.200
Franz Kafka's

00:03:35.200 --> 00:03:36.400
"The Metamorphosis",

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:39.680
in which its workhorse
salesman, Gregor Samsa,

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:41.520
is turned into a giant bug

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:43.600
within the novella's very first sentence,

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:45.600
and has to confront the reality

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:49.200
of his otherwise ordinary
life as a massive bug;

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:51.600
and more than 100 years later,

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:54.160
it is still relatable
as a surreal commentary

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:55.920
on the grind of existence.

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:58.240
But the major turning
point was Roh's writings

00:03:58.240 --> 00:04:00.800
on art being translated into Spanish

00:04:00.800 --> 00:04:02.720
with the assistance of major Spanish

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:04.320
philosopher and essayist,

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:05.840
José Ortega y Gasset.

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:08.400
And from there, as Latin
American writers were traveling

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:11.680
between their home countries
and European cultural hubs,

00:04:11.680 --> 00:04:14.800
authors began cheerleading
the genre and publishing works

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:17.840
that formed the basis of the
genre across the continent,

00:04:17.840 --> 00:04:20.080
like Jorge Luis Borges' 1935

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:22.000
"A Universal History of Infamy"

00:04:22.000 --> 00:04:23.680
and Alejo Carpentier's

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:25.200
"The Kingdom of This World".

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:28.000
In the 1960s, however, magical realism

00:04:28.000 --> 00:04:30.400
finally came to the
forefront of world literature

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:33.040
with Latin America's
Boom Period of writing,

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:35.760
where authors like Gabriel García Márquez

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:37.680
first came to international prominence

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:40.400
with perhaps the most famous
magical realist novel,

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:44.000
his 1967 opus "One Hundred
Years of Solitude".

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:46.080
Written by Márquez in 1967

00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:47.920
translated into 46 languages

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:50.560
with over 57 million copies sold,

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:52.080
"One Hundred Years of Solitude"

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:55.440
tells the multi-generational
story of the Buendía family,

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:58.240
whose patriarch, José Arcadio Buendía,

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:00.000
founded the fictional Colombian town

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:03.040
of Macondo after having
seen it in a dream.

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:04.560
The narrative follows roughly

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:06.720
seven generations of
the family in Macondo,

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:09.680
from their origins in an
isolated, primitive village,

00:05:09.680 --> 00:05:14.480
to civil war, to striking,
to state-sponsored massacres,

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:18.800
to ultimately ending up a ghost
town wiped from existence.

00:05:18.800 --> 00:05:20.560
Suffice to say, it's A LOT.

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:23.760
While this is far from
Márquez's only dalliance

00:05:23.760 --> 00:05:26.880
into magical realism, like
"Love in the Time of Cholera",

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:28.480
it's his most notable.

00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:30.560
But its intensity and bold embracing

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:33.200
of both the supernatural
and the historical

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:35.200
struck audiences immediately.

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:36.560
Said author Toni Morrison

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:38.960
upon reading "One Hundred
Years" for the first time,

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:40.960
"There was something so
familiar about the novel,

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:42.880
so recognizable to me.

00:05:42.880 --> 00:05:45.760
It was a certain kind of
freedom, a structural freedom,

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:48.720
a different notion of a
beginning, middle, and end.

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:50.640
Culturally, I felt intimate with him

00:05:50.640 --> 00:05:53.600
because he was happy to mix
the living and the dead.

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:55.520
His characters were on intimate terms

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:57.200
with the supernatural world,

00:05:57.200 --> 00:05:59.840
and that's the way stories
were told in my house."

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:01.840
Author Pablo Neruda referred to it

00:06:01.840 --> 00:06:04.960
as "The greatest revelation
in the Spanish language

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:07.200
since Don Quixote of Cervantes".

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:09.040
So what makes it magical realism?

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:11.920
Besides, you know, it being
about a town discovered

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:14.640
through dreams and people
flying up into the sky?

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:17.040
It's not just the emphasis on dreams,

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:19.040
but on the mixing of real aspects

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:20.960
of Colombian culture and history,

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:24.240
with Márquez alluding to
or directly folding in

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:28.160
events like the Thousand Days'
War and the Banana Massacre.

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:30.800
Then there are the
"impossible" things happening.

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:32.880
the last surviving Buendía's son,

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:35.120
a product of unknowing incest,

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:37.840
being born with a tail
or, more infamously,

00:06:37.840 --> 00:06:40.320
Remedios Buendía, the
beauty of the family,

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:43.360
rising up into heaven one
day while folding laundry.

00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:45.600
Said Yale Professor Emeritus of Hispanic

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:47.120
and Comparative Literature

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:49.120
Roberto González Echevarría,

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:50.720
"In 'One Hundred Years of Solitude'

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:52.560
myth and history overlap.

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:54.400
The myth acts as a vehicle

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:56.560
to transmit history to the reader.

00:06:56.560 --> 00:06:58.800
García Márquez's novel can furthermore

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:01.200
be referred to as anthropology,

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:03.360
where truth is found in language and myth.

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:06.720
What is real and what is
fiction are indistinguishable."

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:08.960
The Buendías' lives and magical exploits

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:11.520
are so entwined with
the history of Colombia

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:13.120
and the very land itself,

00:07:13.120 --> 00:07:15.680
that the fantastical
and real blend together

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:17.520
into one liminal space,

00:07:17.520 --> 00:07:20.560
where the false heightens the
drama and pain of real life

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:23.120
into the magical, and real life becomes

00:07:23.120 --> 00:07:26.400
a place to ground the mysteries
of things inexplicable.

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:27.920
Said Author Salman Rushdie

00:07:27.920 --> 00:07:31.520
in his 2014 New York Times
obituary for Márquez,

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:35.600
"We live in an age of
invented, alternate worlds.

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:38.480
Tolkien's Middle-earth,
Rowling's Hogwarts,

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:40.640
the dystopic universe
of 'The Hunger Games',

00:07:40.640 --> 00:07:43.440
the places where vampires
and zombies prowl:

00:07:43.440 --> 00:07:45.600
These places are having their day.

00:07:45.600 --> 00:07:48.320
Yet in spite of the vogue
for fantasy fiction,

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:51.280
in the finest of literature's
fictional microcosms

00:07:51.280 --> 00:07:53.600
there is more truth than fantasy.

00:07:53.600 --> 00:07:56.640
In the Macondo of Gabriel García Márquez,

00:07:56.640 --> 00:07:59.520
imagination is used to enrich reality,

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:00.880
not to escape from it."

00:08:00.880 --> 00:08:03.760
To this day, the genre
continues to be the source

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:07.280
of some of contemporary
literature's most popular authors,

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:11.760
like Isabel Allende, Toni
Morrison, and Salman Rushdie.

00:08:11.760 --> 00:08:14.800
While magical realism is a
movement that spans continents,

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:17.440
it is most often associated
with Latin America.

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:18.720
Some writers have argued

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:21.040
the uniqueness of Latin American history

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:23.920
has played a critical role
in defining the genre,

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:26.880
with Alejo Carpentier arguing in his essay

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:28.960
"On the Marvelous Real in America":

00:08:28.960 --> 00:08:30.800
"Because of the virginity of the land,

00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:33.120
our upbringing, our ontology,

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:36.240
the Faustian presence of the
Indian and the Black man,

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:39.360
the revelation constituted
by its recent discovery,

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:41.280
its fecund racial mixing,

00:08:41.280 --> 00:08:44.720
America is far from using up
its wealth of mythologies.

00:08:44.720 --> 00:08:47.760
After all, what is the
entire history of America

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:50.320
if not a chronicle of
the marvellous real?"

00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:52.560
Magical realism as a genre has been used

00:08:52.560 --> 00:08:55.600
to reflect the hard,
ugly truth of real life,

00:08:55.600 --> 00:08:58.320
but it is also been used
to find the extraordinary

00:08:58.320 --> 00:08:59.680
in the small moments,

00:08:59.680 --> 00:09:02.560
and these enduring qualities
have made their home

00:09:02.560 --> 00:09:04.400
in a new generation of writers,

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:08.080
from examples like Silvia
Moreno-Garcia's "Signal to Noise"

00:09:08.080 --> 00:09:10.560
and Ta-Nehisi Coates' "The Water Dancer",

00:09:10.560 --> 00:09:14.640
to Zoraida Córdova's "The
Inheritance of Orquídea Divina".

00:09:14.640 --> 00:09:17.360
This is to say nothing of
magical realism's impacts

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:20.240
on other forms of media,
in particular, filmmaking.

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:22.800
From "Amélie" to "Pan's Labyrinth",

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:25.680
to Disney's newest
animated feature "Encanto",

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:28.320
which openly is a love-letter to Márquez

00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:30.960
and Latin American authors
of the genre itself,

00:09:30.960 --> 00:09:33.120
magical realism is not going anywhere

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:34.480
and that is why it is important

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:36.320
to not just honor the genre,

00:09:36.320 --> 00:09:37.840
but the people it comes from

00:09:37.840 --> 00:09:39.840
and the reality of
which they are speaking.

00:09:39.840 --> 00:09:42.080
So to end with another Rushdie quote,

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:44.720
"When people use the term magic realism,

00:09:44.720 --> 00:09:46.800
usually they only mean 'magic'

00:09:46.800 --> 00:09:48.560
and they don't hear 'realism',

00:09:48.560 --> 00:09:51.520
whereas the way in which
magic realism actually works

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:54.080
is for the magic to be rooted in the real.

00:09:54.080 --> 00:09:55.440
It's both things.

00:09:55.440 --> 00:09:57.680
It's not just a fairytale moment,

00:09:57.680 --> 00:10:00.800
it's the surrealism that
arises out of the real."

